# Chaos: hit flash tint (O224; client/BASE.md "0142"; src/client/chaoshitflash.h (new, pure), creature.h/.cpp,
# luafunctions_client.cpp, mods/client_settings/classes/dataset.lua; after 0001-0141).
# The shader flash of base patch 0126 (80 ms, hard, started by the damage number) stays. This adds a soft tint that follows
# the health instead: when the health percent of a creature on the map goes down, its drawn pieces are queued once more as
# a white silhouette with a falling alpha for 120 ms (ease-out, (1 - u)^2), then it is normal again.
#  * Creature::setHealthPercent calls startHitTint(old, new) before the value is stored. It starts a flash only when
#    chaos::hitflash::wantsFlash allows it: option on, creature on a map tile, health went down, new health above 0 (a
#    dying creature has the death fade of 0139), and the creature was already shown: m_removed is true until a creature is
#    on the map, so the health that comes with its appearance, or with its return into view, is a starting value and never a
#    hit. Heals do not flash.
#  * Strength: 35% white for a 1% hit up to 75% for a 25% hit or more (peakForDrop). Per creature a flash cannot restart
#    inside 100 ms, and over all creatures at most 16 are alive at once (Budget, a fixed array); an area spell on a big pack
#    flashes the first 16 and skips the rest.
#  * Creature::draw: after the outfit is queued, one Color(255, 255, 255, alpha) DrawQueue::setMark over the items queued
#    since the outfit started, the same call the creature mark (m_marked) uses: it redraws the textured rects (the outfit and
#    mount pieces) with the solid-colour-on-texture program, so the outline (0136), the hover outline, the shadow and the
#    names are not touched, and the death fade ghost (0139) is a copy that has no tint. While the shader flash of 0126 runs
#    the tint waits for it, so the two never stack.
#  * Creature.setHitTint(bool) / isHitTint() (static, one switch; off in the engine, the overlay module game_visualfx turns
#    it on from the existing option `hitFlash`).
#  * dataset.lua: the existing option `hitFlash` (default on, checkbox "Flash creatures that take damage" since 0126) gets
#    an apply that tells game_visualfx (`onOption('hitFlash', value)`). No new option, no new text, the panel keeps 276 px.
# Cost with no flash: one int compare per drawn creature and one flag test per health change. With a flash: one extra pass
# over the pieces of that creature's outfit for 120 ms, at most 16 creatures.
# Known: a damage-over-time tick that lowers the percent flashes too (the shader flash skips ticks; this one cannot tell).
# A hit smaller than the 1% step of the health bar is not seen at all. Layered outfits (colour templates) are redrawn through
# the atlas silhouette like a marked creature.
# Checked: applies after 0001-0141 on the base tree; the touched .cpp files pass g++ -fsyntax-only; the header passes its test.
